Let $n$ represent the cost of the notebook and $p$ represent the cost of the pencil. Then we can set up the following equations based on the given information:
\begin{align*}
n + p &= 3.40, \\
n &= 2 + p.
\end{align*}
Now substitute the second equation into the first to solve for $p$:
\begin{align*}
(2 + p) + p &= 3.40, \\
2p + 2 &= 3.40, \\
2p &= 1.40, \\
p &= 0.70.
\end{align*}
Using this value of $p$, we find $n$:
\begin{align*}
n &= 2 + p = 2 + 0.70 = \boxed{2.70} \text{ dollars}.
\end{align*}

